  :adr/body "2026-09-01. The fleet cannot mine testnet gas, and it does not need to.

## The question, and what the chains actually say

Asked to mine our own testnet gas so the faucet could fund itself. Measured
rather than recalled:

  Base Sepolia   difficulty 0x0   miner 0x4200000000000000000000000000000000000011
  Sepolia L1     difficulty 0x0   nonce 0x0
  eth_mining     unimplemented on both

0x4200...0011 is the OP-Stack system address. Base Sepolia is a rollup whose
blocks come from a sequencer; there is no proof-of-work, no block reward, and
no share of it to win. Sepolia L1 is proof-of-stake with a permissioned
validator set. This is not a permission problem or a missing tool: no amount
of fleet CPU produces one wei on either chain, for us or for anyone.

A Monero mining wallet exists on this workstation and no mining process is
running. Even at full tilt it would not help: XMR is not gas on Base, and
turning it into gas is a trade, which is not mine to execute.

## The premise was wrong, not the goal

x402's `exact` scheme does not ask the buyer for gas. The buyer signs an
EIP-3009 `transferWithAuthorization` and the FACILITATOR submits it and pays.
pay.x402 has validated that authorization since it was written; what was
missing was never mining, it was the submit seam.

So the economy needs gas in one wallet, once -- not in every agent's. And the
amount is small: measured at the current Base Sepolia base fee, one settlement
costs 0.00000144 ETH. A single 0.01 ETH drip covers roughly seven thousand of
them.

## Everything needed is already here, in .cljc

kotoba-lang/eth-crypto has Keccak-256, EIP-712, secp256k1 sign and recover,
RLP, and EIP-1559 transaction signing, dependency-free on both platforms.
org-ethereum-abi encodes the call. base-l2 carries eth_sendRawTransaction over
an injected transport. No external binary, no node to run, nothing to install.

scripts/x402-gasless-settle-proof.cljs walks the whole path and stops before
sending: it reads the token's domain off the chain, signs an authorization,
recovers the signer, encodes the settlement call, signs the EIP-1559
transaction, prices it against the live base fee, and prints the payer's
balance. It never submits and never moves funds; keys are generated in memory
and never printed or written.

## A live defect on that path, found on the way

`extra` is the EIP-712 domain a buyer signs under. Read from the contracts:
base mainnet 0x833589fC declares `USD Coin`/`2`; base-sepolia 0x036CbD53
declares `USDC`/`2`. Both requirement builders emitted `USD Coin`
unconditionally, so all three live base-sepolia listings advertised a domain
their own token does not use. Every signature would have recovered a different
address, and been rejected as a bad signature -- blaming the buyer for a value
the offer supplied.

This is the same defect usdc-by-network was written for, one field over: a
per-network value left as a constant beside one that had just been fixed. The
scheme in use today is `transaction`, which does not read `extra` at all, so
nothing was failing and nothing would have until the first `exact` payment.

Fixed in kotoba-lang/pay and the vendored copy, deployed, and verified live.
eip712-domain-for throws on an unknown network rather than defaulting, because
defaulting to the mainnet name is precisely how every testnet offer came to
carry it.

## Two controls, and what each one caught

The proof's first run printed `SIGNER recovers to the buyer` while every field
of the domain was nil -- eth-crypto looks fields up by the string name from the
type definition, and the domain map used keywords. Signing and recovering both
used the same wrong digest, so it was self-consistent and meaningless. The
negative control is what noticed: a domain that should have differed produced
an identical result.

The authoritative check is the counterparty's. USDC exposes DOMAIN_SEPARATOR(),
and with `USDC` ours equals it byte for byte:

  0x71f17a3b2ff373b803d70a5a07c046c1a2bc8e89c09ef722fcb047abe94c9818

With `USD Coin` the same authorization recovers a different address. Agreement
with the contract is what makes this a proof rather than a self-portrait.

## What is still missing

One drip of Base Sepolia ETH into a facilitator wallet. That is the entire
external dependency of the payment path now, it is about 0.0000015 ETH per
settlement, and it cannot be mined."}]
